WHAT WAS ASKED FOR, AND WHAT IS HERE INSTEAD

The ask was for the floor to change live under a camera, described as "REAL ai
generated change of the floor". What this ships renders, live, every floor
Ecowoods can supply and install — and generates nothing.

That is not a shortfall. It is the rule render.ts has carried since the studio
shipped: a diffusion model asked for "white oak herringbone" produces a
beautiful floor that exists nowhere, and the homeowner who falls in love with it
cannot buy it. A generated floor also breaks the one thing this repository is
for, because the price under the picture would belong to no product.

So, per frame: the floor plane is found, a mask decides which of those pixels
are floor rather than furniture, and a configuration from the catalogue is drawn
into them through an exact projective map that keeps the room's own light.
Change species, finish, pattern or width while the camera runs and the room
changes with you, and the installed range changes with it. The claim is narrower
than "AI generates your floor" and, unlike that one, it is true.

IT COULD NOT SHIP ON AV-01 AND AV-02, SO BOTH ARE CLOSED HERE

Pointing a camera at a room made both impossible to look away from: the first
frame repainted a strip along the bottom and left the rest of the floor alone,
and what it did paint went over the dog.

AV-01, the mask. Chroma-only becomes: a per-row luminance profile fitted as a
line, so a floor's falloff with depth is removed before anything is judged; a
dominant-surface mode on the residual, chosen by MASS after smoothing, because a
flat rug otherwise out-votes a textured floor one bin at a time; scale taken
from the mode's core rather than its span; morphological close; and a
4-connected flood that gives back whatever an object encloses. Thirteen of
fourteen fixture objects preserved, against eight before — the jute rug, the
person's leg, the golden retriever, the table leg and the cardboard box all
survive now.

The fourteenth is named rather than buried. An oak stair riser on an oak floor
is the same wood at a different angle to the light, and the rule that catches it
— any luminance departure is an object — also refuses to paint a pool of
sunlight, which leaves a blotch of the old floor in the middle of the new one in
every daylit room. render.test.ts caught exactly that: a satin floor and a matte
floor stopped differing, because the lit pool the sheen shows up in was the part
being skipped. So same chroma with different brightness is treated as LIGHT. The
riser is the price, it is in the audit, and the test asserts it by name so that
neither a regression nor a silent fix can pass.

AV-02, the boundary. The floor is a connected REGION now, grown from the bottom
edge over cells matching the dominant surface of the bottom band, so a rug is a
hole in the floor rather than the end of it. Coverage error on a room with a
rug: 72% to 3%. And the quad is checked for FIT in both directions against the
region's own row extents — a trapezoid stretched over a diagonal wall line, or a
wedge that has missed most of the floor, both report `weak` and hand over the
four corners, which is what the verification screen is for.

THE RENDER PATH HAD TO BE 7.8× FASTER, AND NOTHING ABOUT IT CHANGED

64 ms a frame at 320×240 became 8.2. 63,530 assertions of existing behaviour,
unchanged. Three causes, all of them work that never depended on the pixel:
woodColourAt did a catalogue lookup, two hex parses, a second lookup and an
rgba() string parse PER PIXEL; the grain was six Math.sin per pixel, two of them
constant across a whole board; and relativeLuminance was three Math.pow per
pixel over 256 possible inputs. A memoised palette, a per-board cache, a sine
table for grain only, an sRGB table, and the mask's own cell grid standing in
for four cross products of inQuad on every interior pixel.

WHAT THE LIVE VIEW IS NOT ALLOWED TO SAY

It never says `measured`. The still-photograph path gives a person four corners
to drag and a boundary they have looked at; the live path has neither, and
live.test.ts asserts that the words measured, accurate, exact and precise never
appear in a caption.

PRIVACY, UNCHANGED AND NOW LOAD-BEARING

No upload in the component, no endpoint for one. The stream is attached to a
<video> that never leaves the page, frames are read into an ImageData and
dropped, and the tracks stop on close AND on visibilitychange — a camera light
still on after someone switches apps is what makes a person uninstall a product.
Pointing a camera at a living room makes zero requests, from the network panel.

REACHABLE, AND BRANDED IN THE PIXELS

/floor-studio#live opens the camera, linked from the header panel, the mobile
drawer, ⌘K and the homepage — four surfaces, one entry, because NAV-03 made
lib/navigation.ts the single source. The fragment has a real id="live" behind
it so it lands correctly without JavaScript; verify-destinations required that
and was right to.

The mark, the floor's name, the range and ecowoods.ca are drawn INTO the frame
rather than into HTML around it, because a screenshot of a live camera view is
the most shared artefact this feature will produce and a screenshot carries no
DOM. The host comes from SITE_URL, never a typed string.

Verified: 66 guards pass. 65,361 assertions across the floor-studio and
navigation suites, 0 failed — 63,530 of them the existing renderer, untouched.
53 new assertions on the live logic, which is pure functions over numbers
precisely because a frame loop cannot be tested and a number can.

Still open: GC-026 — the studio has no concept of country, and 26 New York
markets are one tap from a Canadian-dollar range. GEO-006 closes it.

